  "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈0.982\\,79$ rounded to five decimal places\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x≈4.124\\,39$ rounded to five decimal places\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx≈0.982\\,79,\\,x≈4.124\\,39.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈0.982\\,79$ and $x≈4.124\\,39.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (0,2\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}≈0.982\\,79$ and ${x}_{2}≈4.124\\,39.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈0.982\\,79\\cdot 4.124\\,39≈4.05\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places",
